PLACES=[
N('santa-fe-trail-remains-dodge-city','Santa Fe Trail Remains (Dodge City ruts)','Dodge City, Kansas','ks','landmark','traces',37.79194,-100.19694,'site','public','walk','','1821–1880',
  'The Santa Fe Trail ran from Missouri to Santa Fe from 1821 until the railroads replaced it about 1880, and few stretches of it survive. West of Dodge City a two-mile run of ruts crosses 140 acres of old farm and grazing land; it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1963. The Kansas Historical Society calls it the longest continuous stretch of clearly defined trail ruts in Kansas.',
  'A band of parallel swales up to about 400 feet wide curving across the prairie, best seen in low light. US 50, a railroad, irrigation ditches and past grazing cross or wear it in places (Wikipedia).',
  'The longest clear stretch of Santa Fe Trail ruts left in Kansas, a short walk from US 50.',
  'About nine miles west of Dodge City, just off US 50. The Kansas Historical Society says the 140 acres were preserved by the Boot Hill Association and made open to the public; Wikipedia says the site is seen from a pull-off on US 50 and is owned by a preservation organization. Where the pull-off is exactly was not checked on the ground; the federal public-land map does not show the land as public, so go by the society\'s statement.',
  [W('Santa Fe Trail Remains'),['Kansas Historical Society: Ford County register listings',KSR%('2','FO')]],
  brick='no'),
N('mueller-schmidt-house-dodge-city','Mueller-Schmidt House (Home of Stone)','Dodge City, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',37.75557,-100.015546,'exact','open','drive','','1879–1881',
  'John Mueller, a German immigrant boot maker, had limestone hauled from Sawlog Creek in 1879 and moved into his new house in 1881 after eighteen months of building; a German stonemason, William Hessman, cut the stone and another German, William Strubel, did the woodwork. The blacksmith Adam Schmidt and his wife Elizabeth bought it with its furniture in 1890, and the family lived there until Elizabeth died in 1938. It is the only limestone house in Dodge City.',
  'A Gothic Revival house of colour-picked limestone with walls 22 inches thick. The Ford County Historical Society shows the parlour, basement kitchen and bedrooms as they were in 1881.',
  'Dodge City\'s one limestone house, built in 1881 by a boot maker and still furnished as it was.',
  '112 East Vine Street, Dodge City. The Ford County Historical Society runs it as the Home of Stone Museum; its newsletter speaks of it opening for special tours and events (a Christmas tea in December). No regular hours were found on the society\'s pages: hours not checked.',
  [['Ford County Historical Society: the Home of Stone','https://fordcountyhistory.org/?p=7402'],['HMdb: The Mueller-Schmidt House, 1881','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=268935'],['Kansas Historical Society: Ford County register listings',KSR%('2','FO')]],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('ness-county-bank-ness-city','Ness County Bank, the "Skyscraper of the Plains"','Ness City, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.454167,-99.905278,'exact','view','drive','','1888–1890',
  'Built from 1888 to 1890 for the Ness County Bank, the three-storey stone block at Main Street and Pennsylvania Avenue was called on completion the finest building west of Topeka. Later tenants included the Citizens\' State Bank and the post office. It stood empty for ten years until a foundation bought it in 1984 and repaired the roof, stonework and windows.',
  'Limestone walls with the second-floor windows set in great round arches rising from the first floor, and a corner tower with a pyramid roof and four cut-stone spires; the mortar was made with crushed limestone to match the stone. The west part of the main floor holds the Chamber of Commerce office and the east part a restored Great Room used for exhibits and meetings (Chamber of Commerce). When it is open to callers was not checked.',
  'A three-storey 1890 bank tower standing alone over a small High Plains town.',
  'Main Street and Pennsylvania Avenue, Ness City; street parking. Owned by the Ness County Bank Building Foundation (Ness County Chamber of Commerce page). Seen from the street; hours of the Chamber office not checked.',
  [W('Ness County Bank'),['Ness County Chamber: Skyscraper of the Plains','https://www.nesscountychamber.com/skyscraper-of-the-plains.html']],
  brick='no'),
N('pond-creek-station-wallace','Pond Creek stage station (Fort Wallace Museum)','Wallace, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.910351,-101.583243,'site','open','drive','','1865',
  'A two-storey frame stage station built in 1865 for the Butterfield Overland Despatch, believed to be the oldest building made by settlers in north-west Kansas and the only building of that stage line left in Kansas. Fort Wallace began beside it as Camp Pond Creek, to guard the stage road. The station has been moved more than once, in 1871 and 1898, and stands about a mile from where it was built (the museum) or half a mile (Wikipedia).',
  'The wooden station, which Wikipedia says carries bullet holes from attacks, stands just west of the main Fort Wallace Museum building. Of the fort itself only the cemetery is left. Interior not checked.',
  'The last stage station of the Butterfield Overland Despatch, beside a frontier fort museum.',
  'Fort Wallace Museum, 2655 Highway 40, Wallace. Admission free, with a suggested donation of $10 a person (the museum\'s own page).',
  [W('Pond Creek Station'),['Fort Wallace Museum: about and hours','https://ftwallace.com/pages/about-us/'],['Fort Wallace Museum: collection','https://ftwallace.com/collection/']],
  brick='no',hours='Mid March to October: Mon–Sat 9 a.m.–5 p.m., Sun 1–5 p.m. November to mid March: Mon–Sat 10 a.m.–4 p.m.'),
N('old-logan-county-courthouse-russell-springs','Old Logan County Courthouse (Butterfield Trail Museum)','Russell Springs, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.91236,-101.18049,'exact','open','drive','','1887',
  'Russell Springs built this courthouse in 1887, designed by Alfred Meyer and put up by a local builder, George D. Kerns; the town is now small and the county seat is elsewhere. The building houses the Butterfield Trail Museum, about the area\'s pioneers, the plains and the Butterfield Overland Despatch stage line.',
  'A Renaissance Revival courthouse on Main Street. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1887 courthouse in a town of a few dozen people, now a stage-line museum.',
  'Main Street, Russell Springs. Wikipedia says the museum opens seasonally; no page of its own could be read, so hours not checked.',
  [W('Old Logan County Courthouse (Kansas)')],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('old-greeley-county-courthouse-tribune','Old Greeley County Courthouse (Horace Greeley Museum)','Tribune, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.47147,-101.75045,'exact','open','drive','','1889–1890',
  'Greeley County was formed in 1888 and began its first courthouse a year later, finishing it in 1890 to a design by William T. Heaps, who also designed Hamilton County\'s courthouse. The county moved into a new building beside it in 1975, and the old one became the historical society\'s museum, now named the Horace Greeley Museum.',
  'A plain stone courthouse with an iron cornice and a clock tower. Wikipedia calls the stone sandstone; the museum\'s own page calls it limestone. Interior not checked.',
  'The first courthouse of a far-west Kansas county, kept as its museum.',
  '214 East Harper Street, Tribune, run by the Greeley County Historical Society. Tribune keeps Mountain Time. The museum says to call on a Thursday to arrange a tour on another day.',
  [W('Horace Greeley Museum'),['Horace Greeley Museum','https://horacegreeleymuseum.org'],['Unified Greeley County: the museum','https://www.greeleycountyks.gov/community/museum.html']],
  brick='no',hours='Thursdays 9 a.m. to 3 p.m. Mountain Time; other days by arrangement'),
N('wagon-bed-spring-ulysses','Wagon Bed Spring (Lower Cimarron Spring)','Ulysses, Kansas','ks','landmark','traces',37.39833,-101.37083,'area','view','walk','','1820s–1860s',
  'The Lower Spring on the Cimarron River was the first sure water on the Cimarron Route of the Santa Fe Trail after leaving the Arkansas, and traders and soldiers camped here for some sixty years; the mountain man Jedediah Smith was probably killed near it. Cowboys of the cattle-drive era sank an old wagon bed in it, which gave the later name. Irrigation lowered the water table and the spring stopped flowing in the 1960s. It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1961.',
  'Interpretive signs and a marker on what was the north bank of the Cimarron; the spring itself is dry and now lies in the river bed (Wikipedia). Expect open ground more than a feature.',
  'The dry spring where Santa Fe Trail wagons on the Cimarron Route first found water.',
  'About 12 miles south of Ulysses, on the west side of K-25 (Wikipedia). Who owns the ground and how far the public may walk was not checked: look from the signs.',
  [W('Wagon Bed Spring'),['NPS: Santa Fe Trail sites to the west and south (PDF)','https://www.nps.gov/safe/learn/historyculture/upload/Santa-Fe-Trail-Sites-to-the-West-508.pdf'],['High Plains Public Radio: Wagon Bed Springs','https://www.hppr.org/2026-01-13/high-plains-history-wagon-bed-springs']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('carry-nation-home-medicine-lodge','Carry Nation Home','Medicine Lodge, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',37.27562,-98.582,'exact','open','drive','','lived in 1889–1902',
  'The temperance crusader Carry Nation lived in this small brick house from 1889 to 1902; in 1899, after what she called a heavenly vision, she went from here to Kiowa and wrecked three saloons. She sold the house in 1902 to open a home in Kansas City for drunkards\' wives. The Women\'s Christian Temperance Union bought it in the 1950s, and it was made a National Historic Landmark in 1976.',
  'A small brick house at the corner of Fowler Avenue (US 160) and Oak Street, next to a 1961 copy of the town\'s 1874 stockade that is now the Stockade Museum. Interior not checked.',
  'The house a hatchet-swinging temperance crusader set out from to smash saloons.',
  '211 West Fowler Avenue, Medicine Lodge. A ticket to the Stockade Museum also admits you to the Carry Nation Home (the Peace Treaty association\'s page). Regular hours not found: hours not checked.',
  [W('Carry Nation Home'),['Medicine Lodge Peace Treaty: the Stockade','https://peacetreaty.org/stockade']],
  brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fromme-birney-round-barn','Fromme-Birney Round Barn','Mullinville, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',37.52668,-99.50894,'exact','open','drive','','1912',
  'Henry Fromme, a German immigrant, paid the carpenter William "Pat" Campbell $8,000 in 1912 to build a round barn for 28 draft horses and a Percheron stallion. Tractors soon made it obsolete and it stored hay; in the Second World War it served as a landmark for bomber navigators in training. Phyllis Birney gave it to the Kiowa County Historical Society, which restored it.',
  'Not round but sixteen-sided, 50 feet tall and 70 feet across, with a domed roof, a sixteen-sided cupola and a weathervane; inside, a sixteen-sided granary in the middle, a wagon ring and fourteen stalls, and a two-level haymow. Displays on round barns and 1912 farm life (Kansas Sampler Foundation).',
  'A sixteen-sided 1912 horse barn standing alone on the plains, open every day.',
  'Owned and run by the Kiowa County Historical Society. From US 54 at Mullinville go 3.5 miles south, then 1.75 miles west (Kansas Sampler Foundation). The society\'s own site could not be opened when this was written.',
  [W('Fromme-Birney Round Barn'),['Kansas Sampler Foundation: Fromme-Birney Barn','https://kansassampler.org/8wondersofkansas-architecture/fromme-birney-barn-mullinville']],
  brick='no',hours='Open daily; donations'),
N('big-well-greensburg','Big Well','Greensburg, Kansas','ks','water','standing',37.60556,-99.29361,'exact','open','walk','','1887–1888',
  'Dug by hand in 1887 and 1888 for $45,000 to supply the Santa Fe and Rock Island railroads, the well was Greensburg\'s water supply until 1932. Crews of a dozen or so men dug with picks and shovels and lined the shaft with stone hauled by wagon from the Medicine River. The building over it was destroyed in the 2007 tornado that flattened the town, and a new museum was built over the well.',
  'A stone-lined shaft you go down by a lit stairway, for a fee (Wikipedia).',
  'A hand-dug railroad well you can climb down into.',
  'The Big Well Museum, Greensburg. Its own site is drawn by script and could not be read: hours and fees not checked.',
  [W('Big Well (Kansas)')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('elk-falls-pratt-truss-bridge','Elk Falls Pratt Truss Bridge','Elk Falls, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',37.37418,-96.18412,'exact','public','walk','','1892 or 1893',
  'An iron Pratt truss bridge over the Elk River at the east end of Montgomery Street, built by the George E. King Bridge Company: Wikipedia gives 1892, the marker at the bridge 1893. It was taken out of service for cars in the 1970s and kept for people on foot; the marker says it has been repaired and re-planked with help from Coffeyville Community College. The town is named for the falls on the river beside it.',
  'The truss and its plank deck, with a view of the falls from the bridge (HMdb photo caption).',
  'An 1890s iron truss over the falls that gave a tiny town its name.',
  'East end of Montgomery Street, Elk Falls; park in the street. A public street bridge closed to cars, by Wikipedia; who owns it now was not checked. The bridge was open to walkers when the marker and Wikipedia were written; its condition now was not checked.',
  [W('Elk Falls Pratt Truss Bridge'),['HMdb: Elk Falls Pratt Truss Bridge','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=79224']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('old-allen-county-jail-iola','Old Allen County Jail','Iola, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',37.92333,-95.40278,'exact','open','drive','','1868–1869',
  'Voters approved a $10,000 bond in 1868 and the contractors White and Hays finished the jail in October 1869 for $8,400, the county\'s first public building. Called the finest jail in south-east Kansas, it was never secure: many prisoners escaped by pulling bars from windows or cutting through the roof. It served for nearly a century and is now the Old Jail Museum of the Allen County Historical Society.',
  'Two storeys of rubble limestone with walls about two feet thick: the jailer lived upstairs, and the steel-barred cells stood on the stone floor below (Wikipedia).',
  'An 1869 limestone jail that prisoners kept breaking out of.',
  'On the courthouse square in Iola, run by the Allen County Historical Society; tours by appointment or from the times the city\'s page gives.',
  [W('Allen County Jail'),['City of Iola: Old Allen County Jail','https://cityofiola.com/218/Old-Allen-County-Jail']],
  brick='no',hours='Tours Tuesday to Saturday starting 1:30 p.m., or by appointment'),
N('niotaze-methodist-episcopal-church','Niotaze Methodist Episcopal Church','Niotaze, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',37.07,-96.015,'site','view','drive','','about 1895',
  'A one-room church built about 1895 to a design by Benjamin D. Price, a well-known church architect, in the small town of Niotaze. It is also called the Odell Memorial Methodist Episcopal Church and the Niotaze United Methodist Church, and was added to the National Register in 2006.',
  'A front-gabled church of rough-faced sandstone blocks on a sandstone foundation. Interior not checked.',
  'A little 1890s sandstone church by a noted church architect, in a hamlet near the Oklahoma line.',
  '301 North F Street, Niotaze. Whether it still holds services was not checked: look from the street.',
  [W('Niotaze Methodist Episcopal Church')],
  brick='no'),
N('old-sedgwick-county-courthouse-wichita','Old Sedgwick County Courthouse','Wichita, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',37.69418,-97.33755,'site','view','drive','','1888–1959',
  'The contract for the courthouse was let to W. H. Sternberg of Wichita in August 1888 for $197,329, to a design by W. R. McPherson. It served the county for 71 years until a new eleven-storey courthouse replaced it in 1959. In 1970, when it was nominated to the National Register, part of it was used by county offices and the rest stood empty.',
  'Grey native stone with sawn faces on a rough-hewn base, stone bands at each floor and a central tower. The tower was six storeys tall with six-foot clock faces; the top, clock and all, was taken off for safety before 1970 (Register form). Its use today was not checked.',
  'Wichita\'s 1888 stone courthouse, still standing beside its successor.',
  '510 North Main Street, downtown Wichita, across the street from the 1959 courthouse. Seen from the street; who uses it now and whether it opens was not checked.',
  [['National Register form (PDF)',NR%'71000327']],
  brick='no'),
N('wichita-old-city-hall-museum','Old Wichita City Hall (Wichita-Sedgwick County Historical Museum)','Wichita, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',37.68472,-97.33778,'exact','open','drive','','1890',
  'Wichita\'s city hall was designed by William T. Proudfoot and George W. Bird in 1890, in the Romanesque style. Since 1939 it has held the Wichita-Sedgwick County Historical Museum, which has four floors of exhibits on the city and county, among them aviation and a Jones Six automobile made in Wichita.',
  'The 1890 Romanesque city hall and its four exhibit floors. What survives of the original interior was not checked.',
  'Wichita\'s 1890 city hall, now its history museum.',
  '204 South Main Street, Wichita. Admission charged except on Sunday afternoons in 2026, when it is free (the museum\'s page); street and lot parking, free on Sundays.',
  [W('Wichita-Sedgwick County Historical Museum'),['Museum: hours and admission','https://www.wichitahistory.org/hours-admission/'],['Kansas Historical Society: Sedgwick County register listings',KSR%('15','SG')]],
  brick='?',hours='Tue–Fri 11 a.m.–4 p.m., Sat–Sun 1–5 p.m.; closed Mondays and holidays'),
N('condon-bank-coffeyville','Condon National Bank (Perkins Block)','Coffeyville, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',37.035556,-95.615278,'exact','view','walk','','1890',
  'The Perkins Block was built in 1890 to house the C. M. Condon bank. On October 5, 1892 the Dalton gang tried to rob it and another bank across the square at the same time; townspeople armed themselves, and Bob and Grat Dalton, Dick Broadwell and Bill Power were killed by townspeople, the last of them in the alley where the gang had left its horses.',
  'Red brick side walls laid in running bond and a stamped-metal front (Register form). A National Park Service lesson says the building houses the Chamber of Commerce, opens in business hours, and still shows the bank and its vault as they were in 1892; when that was written was not checked. The Dalton Defenders Museum is across the street at 814 Walnut.',
  'The bank where the Dalton gang was shot down in 1892.',
  'On the town square, downtown Coffeyville. Whether you may go inside now was not checked: look from the street. The Dalton Defenders Museum across the street gives its own hours (Coffeyville Historical Society).',
  [['National Register form (PDF)',NR%'73000771'],['NPS: Coffeyville, the town that stopped the Dalton gang','https://www.nps.gov/articles/coffeyville-kansas-the-town-that-stopped-the-dalton-gang-teaching-with-historic-places.htm'],['Coffeyville Historical Society: Dalton Defenders Museum','https://coffeyvillehistory.com/dalton-defenders-museum']],
  conf='mixed',brick='some'),
N('rainbow-bridge-riverton','Rainbow Bridge (Brush Creek Bridge), Route 66','Riverton, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',37.07342,-94.74077,'exact','public','drive','','1923',
  'A single-span concrete Marsh arch bridge built in 1923 over Brush Creek on what became US 66, about two miles west of Riverton. Of three Marsh arches on Route 66 in Kansas it is the only one left on the whole length of the old highway. A new bridge now carries the through traffic, and the old one was kept after the Kansas Route 66 Association objected to its demolition.',
  'A white concrete arch on each side of the deck, often repainted after graffiti (Wikipedia).',
  'The last Marsh rainbow arch on all of Route 66.',
  'On the old Route 66, now a Cherokee County road (Wikipedia), west of Riverton. A short one-way road takes traffic over the old bridge, which may still be driven across (Wikipedia).',
  [W('Rainbow Bridge (Kansas)')],
  brick='no'),
N('home-on-the-range-cabin','Home on the Range Cabin','Smith Center, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.88939,-98.94717,'site','open','drive','','1875',
  'Dr. Brewster Higley wrote the poem that became "Home on the Range" in 1872 while living in a dugout on Beaver Creek, and built this log cabin in 1875. His friend Dan Kelley set it to music, and it is now the state song of Kansas. The cabin was restored to its 1870s look in 2013.',
  'The restored log cabin on 240 acres of range and farmland, with markers and a nature walk nearby (HMdb). Interior not checked.',
  'The cabin of the man who wrote "Home on the Range".',
  'About 12 miles north-west of Smith Center (HMdb). The land is owned by the Peoples Heartland Foundation (Wikipedia); Kansas\'s tourism office says the cabin welcomes visitors daily. The marker says the cabin is behind the trees south of a white house: keep to the visitor area.',
  [W('Home on the Range Cabin'),['HMdb: Higley Cabin','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=52854'],['TravelKS: Kansas is home on the range','https://www.travelks.com/blog/stories/post/kansas-is-home-on-the-range/']],
  brick='no',hours='Daily (TravelKS)'),
N('shirley-opera-house-atwood','Shirley Opera House','Atwood, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',39.80782,-101.0406,'exact','view','drive','','1907',
  'Built in 1907, the same years as the Rawlins County Courthouse, Atwood\'s first permanent sidewalks and its waterworks. Upstairs was the town hall for rallies, graduations, dances, debates, musicals and road shows; the ground floor held shops, among them a department store. It was listed on the National Register in 2007.',
  'A commercial block with Victorian detailing; its Register form calls it the only commercial building of its time in Atwood not much altered. Interior not checked.',
  'A 1907 upstairs opera house on a far-north-west Kansas main street.',
  '503 Main Street, Atwood; street parking. Seen from the street; current use not checked.',
  [W('Shirley Opera House')],
  brick='?'),
]
